            and discussion of properties of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            relevant in this context is at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sufficiently+cohesive+topos"} sufficiently cohesive topos
          %h2#categories_without_subobject_classifiers Categories without subobject classifiers
          %p As the previous section indicates, having a subobject classifier is a very strong property of a category and “most” categories with finite limits don’t have one.
          %p
            For example, there is an easy condition ensuring a category
            %sup#fnref:2
              %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
            with a terminal object can’t have a subobject classifier: if there are no nonidentity morphisms out of the terminal object. This includes the following examples.
          %ul
            %li
              %p Any top bounded partial order.
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ring
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ring
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+rings"} category of rings
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+ring"} zero ring
          %p Here’s another obstacle:
          %ul
            %li
              If an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              had a subobject classifier, every subobject of every object would have to be the kernel of its classifying map. In particular, the subobject
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              of every object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              would have to be the kernel of its classifying map, so that every object in this abelian category would embed into the subobject classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              (including, say, all small products of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              with itself) which in nontrivial cases would cause size issues.
          %p But a real killer is the fact that all monos are regular, or its consequences of the category being balanced and uniqueness of epi-mono factorizations:
          %ul
            %li
              The categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              are not balanced (consider the map from a discrete structure on a set to an indiscrete structure on the same set, induced by the identity function). The category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CMon"} CMon
              is not balanced (consider the inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Z}
              which is epic).
          %p
            Even though all monos in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            are regular, we can kill off
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
            by observing that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t: 1 \to \Omega
            were a subobject classifier, the proof of Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#regular"}
            indicates that every mono
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: A \to X
            would have to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
            are kernels.
          %p Perhaps an even more decisive killer is the observation that meets distribute over (arbitrary) joins in subobject orders. This eliminates many categories from consideration:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Lattices of subobjects in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Grp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
                are rarely distributive.
            %li
              %p
                For any nontrivial category with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
